// Tier 1, chapter 1: kinematics. Words only; the numbers are generated per save.
Chapter(
    id: "1.1",
    tier: 1,
    domain: Mechanics,
    title: "The Salvage Launcher",
    part: "Skiff frame",
    intro: "The frame beam for the skiff lies at the wreck on the south coast. The shipyard is on the north shore, facing the strait, with a canyon and a ridge in between. The wreck came down across a road of pale stone that nobody here built, and along it stand its builders' machines: a cart in a groove, a crawler, a drop tower, two launchers, a quay. All of them are asleep. Wire a console into one and it does exactly what you tell it, no more. Do its job right, and the next one wakes.",
    goal: "Get the frame beam from the wreck to the shipyard on the north shore: up the road, across the canyon, over the ridge.",
    // Lumen's words. Spoken, so no digits; `look` is a place its eye holds while it speaks.
    opening: [
        Spoken(text: "Lumen here, the ship's computer. Some of me: a backup, on this section's cells. I can see, but I have no hands. Those are yours."),
        Spoken(text: "That is the main hull, across the strait. The rest of me is in it, and what we need to leave. We need a boat.", look: Some("The main hull, across the strait")),
        Spoken(text: "A boat starts with a frame. Its beam is here. The shipyard is on the north shore, past the canyon and the ridge."),
        Spoken(text: "And we came down across a road. Pale stone, quartz in the joints, machines along it, all asleep. Not ours, and older than anything I know. If they still work, they can carry the beam for us."),
    ],
    closing: [
        Spoken(text: "That is the whole frame. Come and see it on the shipyard floor.", look: Some("The shipyard floor")),
        Spoken(text: "The gate on the quay is pointing across the strait. And just then, something answered. The core. Faint, but it was there.", look: Some("The north gate")),
    ],
    remarks: [
        Remark(near: "The wreck", radius: 7.0, when: Always, line: Spoken(text: "That was the forward section. This backup of me lives in what is left of it. We came down right across their road. Nobody has complained yet.")),
        Remark(near: "The shore", radius: 10.0, when: Before("1.1.4"), line: Spoken(text: "Still nothing from over there. The core is cold, or my receiver is.", look: Some("The main hull, across the strait"))),
        Remark(near: "The shore", radius: 10.0, when: After("1.1.4"), line: Spoken(text: "The gate is still pointing over there. I do not know what its builders meant by it, but I like where it points.", look: Some("The main hull, across the strait"))),
        Remark(near: "Ledge launcher: release point", radius: 8.0, when: Before("1.1.3"), line: Spoken(text: "A long way down. Nobody is walking a beam across that. Whoever built this launcher thought the same.")),
        Remark(near: "Ridge: foot, near side", radius: 8.0, when: Before("1.1.4"), line: Spoken(text: "The shipyard is just over this, and the strait just past it. Close enough to see, too steep to carry anything up.")),
        Remark(near: "The shipyard floor", radius: 12.0, when: Before("1.1.4"), line: Spoken(text: "An old quay, with a slipway down to the water. They launched something from here once. For now it is a floor, and the skiff goes together on it.", look: Some("The main hull, across the strait"))),
        Remark(near: "The shipyard floor", radius: 12.0, when: After("1.1.4"), line: Spoken(text: "A frame, not a boat yet. It needs power and a hull before it can cross. The rest of me can wait.", look: Some("The main hull, across the strait"))),
    ],
    concepts: [
        Concept(
            id: "units",
            title: "Measurements carry units",
            found: FirstCommit("1.1.0"),
            hint: "Enter a value at the supply rail station and commit it.",
            card: "A measurement is a number together with a unit. 12 m is a length and 1.5 s is a time, but the number 12 on its own could be either.\n\nEvery value you enter at a station must have the kind of unit it asks for. Type the unit after the number (12 m), or use a reading from the notebook by its name (d1): the notebook keeps the unit with the number.\n\nA value of the wrong kind is refused before anything runs, and a refusal costs no charge.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: Tape,
                caption: "On Earth: a sofa measured with a tape. \"2\" on its own could mean anything; \"2 m\" says it is a length.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "Which of these is a time?",
                    choices: ["12 m", "12", "1.5 s", "3 m/s"],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "s is seconds, a unit of time. m is metres (a length), m/s is a speed, and 12 on its own is not a measurement of anything.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A station asks for a length and you type 12. What happens?",
                    choices: ["It is read as 12 m.", "It is refused: 12 could be anything.", "It is read as 12 cm."],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "A bare number has no kind. The station refuses it, says why, and uses no charge. Type 12 m, or a notebook name such as d1.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A road sign says the next town is 3 km away. In metres, that is…",
                    choices: ["3000 m", "300 m", "0.003 m"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "A kilometre is a thousand metres: 3 × 1000 m = 3000 m. The length is the same; only the unit it is counted in changes.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "velocity",
            title: "Velocity",
            found: Demo("1.1.1"),
            hint: "Watch the crawler and the haul cart set off: try the experiment at the Catch the crawler station.",
            card: "Velocity says how fast a position changes, and in which direction. Over a stretch of steady motion it is the distance covered divided by the time taken:\n\n    v = (change in x) / (change in t)\n\nIts unit is a length over a time: m/s. Something at a steady velocity covers equal distances in equal times, so its position grows in equal steps:\n\n    x = x0 + v t\n\nHere x0 is where it was when the clock started.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: Cyclist,
                caption: "On Earth: a cyclist at a steady pace, flashed once a second. The marks are evenly spaced: the same distance in every second.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A cyclist covers 100 m in 20 s at a steady pace. Their velocity is…",
                    choices: ["20 m/s", "5 m/s", "2000 m/s"],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "Distance over time: 100 m / 20 s = 5 m/s. Multiplying instead would give m·s, which is not a speed at all.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "Photos taken once a second show a runner's positions evenly spaced. The runner is…",
                    choices: ["speeding up.", "slowing down.", "moving at a steady velocity."],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "Equal distances in equal times means a steady velocity. Gaps that grew would mean speeding up; gaps that shrank, slowing down.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A train runs at a steady 30 m/s. It passes the 100 m mark as the clock starts. Where is it 4 s later?",
                    choices: ["At 220 m", "At 130 m", "At 120 m"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "x = x0 + v t = 100 m + 30 m/s × 4 s = 100 m + 120 m = 220 m. Don't forget where it started.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "relative_velocity",
            title: "Relative velocity",
            found: Demo("1.1.1"),
            hint: "Watch the crawler and the haul cart set off: try the experiment at the Catch the crawler station.",
            card: "Two things move the same way along a line, at steady velocities v1 and v2. The gap between them changes at the difference:\n\n    v1 - v2\n\nThat is the velocity of one as seen from the other. The gap changes by the same amount every second: it shrinks if the one behind is faster, and grows if the one in front is. Moving towards each other, two things close the gap at v1 + v2.\n\nA chaser a distance D behind, moving at v1, catches up with what it chases (moving at v2) after the time\n\n    t = D / (v1 - v2)",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: Overtake,
                caption: "On Earth: a car catching up with a bus. The gap between them shrinks by the same amount every second.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A car at 25 m/s follows a bus at 15 m/s, both going the same way. The gap between them shrinks by…",
                    choices: ["40 m every second", "25 m every second", "10 m every second"],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "Same direction: the gap changes at the difference of the velocities, 25 m/s − 15 m/s = 10 m/s.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "Two cyclists ride towards each other at 6 m/s and 4 m/s. The gap between them closes at…",
                    choices: ["10 m/s", "2 m/s", "24 m/s"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "Moving towards each other, both eat into the gap: 6 m/s + 4 m/s = 10 m/s.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A car 50 m behind a bus closes the gap at 10 m/s. It reaches the bus after…",
                    choices: ["500 s", "5 s", "0.2 s"],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "t = D / (v1 − v2) = 50 m / 10 m/s = 5 s. Check the units: m divided by m/s is s.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "acceleration",
            title: "Acceleration",
            found: Demo("1.1.2"),
            hint: "Watch the test mass fall from the drop tower: try the experiment at the Local gravity station.",
            card: "Acceleration says how fast a velocity changes. For something speeding up steadily, it is the change in velocity divided by the time taken:\n\n    a = (change in v) / (change in t)\n\nIts unit is a velocity over a time: (m/s)/s, written m/s². An acceleration of 3 m/s² adds 3 m/s to the speed every second.\n\nStarting from rest with a steady acceleration a, after a time t the velocity and the distance covered are\n\n    v = a t\n    x = (1/2) a t^2\n\nThe speed grows by the same step every second, so the distance covered in each second grows too: photos taken at equal moments show the gaps getting longer.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: GreenLight,
                caption: "On Earth: a car pulling away from traffic lights, flashed once a second. Its speed grows by the same step every second, so each gap is longer than the last.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A car pulls away from traffic lights. Read once a second, its speed is 0, 3, 6, then 9 m/s. Its acceleration is…",
                    choices: ["3 m/s²", "9 m/s²", "18 m/s²"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "The speed rises by 3 m/s in every second: a = 3 m/s / 1 s = 3 m/s². 9 m/s is how fast it ends up going, not how fast its speed changes.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A cyclist speeds up steadily from 2 m/s to 8 m/s in 3 s. Their acceleration is…",
                    choices: ["2 m/s²", "6 m/s²", "18 m/s²"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "a = (change in v) / (change in t) = (8 m/s − 2 m/s) / 3 s = 6 m/s / 3 s = 2 m/s².",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A car starts from rest with a steady acceleration of 2 m/s². How far has it gone after 5 s?",
                    choices: ["10 m", "25 m", "50 m"],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "x = (1/2) a t² = 0.5 × 2 m/s² × (5 s)² = 25 m. Its speed is then a t = 10 m/s, but it started from rest, so it did not go that fast the whole time.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "free_fall",
            title: "Free fall",
            found: Demo("1.1.2"),
            hint: "Watch the test mass fall from the drop tower: try the experiment at the Local gravity station.",
            card: "An object let go from rest, with only gravity pulling on it, falls with a steady acceleration g: the gravitational acceleration, in m/s². After a time t it has fallen\n\n    h = (1/2) g t^2\n\nThe object's mass does not appear: heavy and light things fall together. (Air can hold back light, spread-out things such as feathers, but hardly a crate or an apple.)\n\ng belongs to the planet. This is not the planet you learned its value on: measure it here.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: AppleDrop,
                caption: "On Earth: an apple dropped from a balcony, flashed at equal moments, falls further in each one. In a tube with the air pumped out, a feather keeps up with a hammer.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "Photos taken at equal moments show a falling apple. The gaps between its positions…",
                    choices: ["stay the same.", "grow as it falls.", "shrink as it falls."],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "It gains speed all the way down, so in each moment it falls further than in the one before.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "In a tube with the air pumped out, a feather and a hammer are let go together. Which lands first?",
                    choices: ["The hammer.", "The feather.", "They land together."],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "Mass does not appear in h = (1/2) g t². Only air holds the feather back, and there is none in the tube. Astronauts did this on the Moon.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "On Earth, g is about 9.8 m/s². How far does a stone fall from rest in 2 s?",
                    choices: ["About 20 m", "About 10 m", "About 40 m"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "h = (1/2) g t² = 0.5 × 9.8 m/s² × (2 s)² = 0.5 × 9.8 × 4 m ≈ 19.6 m. Here g is different: it has to be measured.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "independence",
            title: "Horizontal and vertical motion are independent",
            found: Demo("1.1.3"),
            hint: "Watch the two test crates leave the ledge: try the experiment at the Across the canyon station.",
            card: "A thrown object does two things at once, and neither affects the other.\n\nSideways, nothing pushes it, so its horizontal speed stays the same:\n\n    x = vx t\n\nUp and down, gravity acts exactly as in free fall:\n\n    y = vy t - (1/2) g t^2\n\nSo a crate fired horizontally off a ledge and a crate simply dropped from the same ledge reach the ground at the same moment.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: TableEdge,
                caption: "On Earth: a marble rolled off a table and one dropped from its edge. At every flash they are at the same height, and they land together.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A marble rolls off a table at the moment a second marble is dropped from its edge. Which lands first?",
                    choices: ["Both together", "The rolled one", "The dropped one"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "Rolling sideways gives no downward speed. Both start falling from rest at the same height, so both fall for the same time.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "While the rolled marble is in the air, its horizontal speed…",
                    choices: ["grows.", "shrinks to nothing by the floor.", "stays the same."],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "Nothing pushes it sideways, so its horizontal speed stays constant: x = vx t. Gravity only changes the vertical part.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A ball rolls off a table at 2 m/s and is in the air for 0.5 s. How far from the table's edge does it land?",
                    choices: ["2 m", "1 m", "4 m"],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "Sideways, x = vx t = 2 m/s × 0.5 s = 1 m. How long it is in the air depends only on the table's height.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "components",
            title: "Splitting a launch into components",
            found: Demo("1.1.4"),
            hint: "Watch the two test slugs: try the experiment at the Over the ridge station.",
            card: "A launch at speed v and angle theta above the horizontal has a sideways part and an upward part:\n\n    vx = v cos(theta)        vy = v sin(theta)\n\nThe two parts then move independently. The steeper the launch, the more of the speed goes into height and time in the air, and the less into covering ground.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: KickSplit,
                caption: "On Earth: a football kick. Its velocity splits into a sideways part, which stays the same, and an upward part, which gravity uses up and turns around.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A football is kicked at the same speed but more steeply. Its upward part vy…",
                    choices: ["shrinks, and its sideways part grows.", "stays the same.", "grows, and its sideways part shrinks."],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "Steeper means more of the speed points up: sin(theta) grows with the angle while cos(theta) shrinks.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A ball is thrown straight up, at 90 degrees. Its sideways part vx is…",
                    choices: ["zero.", "equal to its speed.", "half its speed."],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "cos(90°) = 0: none of the speed goes sideways, so the ball comes back down where it started.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A kick at 20 m/s, 30 degrees above the ground. Its upward part is… (sin 30° = 0.5)",
                    choices: ["20 m/s", "10 m/s", "40 m/s"],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "vy = v sin(theta) = 20 m/s × 0.5 = 10 m/s. The sideways part is 20 m/s × cos(30°), about 17.3 m/s.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Concept(
            id: "range",
            title: "Range on level ground",
            found: Demo("1.1.4"),
            hint: "Watch the two test slugs: try the experiment at the Over the ridge station.",
            card: "If an object lands at the same height it was launched from, it is in the air for\n\n    t = 2 v sin(theta) / g\n\nand in that time it travels\n\n    R = v^2 sin(2 theta) / g\n\nTwo different angles give the same range: a flat one and a steep one, equally far either side of 45 degrees. Their paths are not the same: the steep one climbs higher and stays up longer.",
            illustration: Some(Illustration(
                scene: TwinKicks,
                caption: "On Earth: two kicks at the same speed, one flat and one steep, land on the same spot. The steep one goes higher and takes longer.",
            )),
            quiz: [
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "On level ground, at the same speed, which angle sends a ball furthest?",
                    choices: ["30 degrees", "45 degrees", "60 degrees"],
                    answer: 1,
                    explain: "R = v² sin(2 theta) / g is largest when sin(2 theta) = 1, that is when 2 theta = 90° and theta = 45°.",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "A kick at 20 degrees lands on a spot. At the same speed, which other angle lands there too?",
                    choices: ["40 degrees", "25 degrees", "70 degrees"],
                    answer: 2,
                    explain: "20 degrees is 25 below 45, and 70 is 25 above. Check: sin(2 × 20°) = sin(40°) = sin(140°) = sin(2 × 70°).",
                ),
                QuizQuestion(
                    question: "Of the two kicks at 30 and 60 degrees that land on the same spot, which is in the air longer?",
                    choices: ["The 60 degree kick", "The 30 degree kick", "Both the same"],
                    answer: 0,
                    explain: "t = 2 v sin(theta) / g: the steeper kick has more upward speed, so it climbs higher and stays up longer, but covers less ground each second.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
    ],
    puzzles: [
        PuzzleDef(
            id: "1.1.0",
            kind: Rail,
            title: "The supply rail",
            task: "Load the rail cart and send it to the orange marker.",
            why: "The first crate out of the wreck: our tools, up the road. Nothing hard here; this is how every station works.",
            done: "The marker lit up when the cart stopped on it, and the stone gate opened. I did nothing. Let us see what is past it.",
            brief: "Every job on this island runs through a station like this one: read what is needed, measure the world, enter values, commit, and watch what happens.\n\nStart with something that needs no physics. The road the wreck came down across was built by someone else, and their machines stand along it, asleep. The first is a cart on a rail sunk into the road towards the outcrop: the supply rail. The console wired into it drives the cart exactly as far as you tell it, then stops; the stone gate at the end of the stretch stops it anyway. Load it, then send it to the orange marker across the rail.\n\nThe cart will not run empty. Its cargo, a crate of our tools from the wreck, stands beside the rail: leave the console, walk up to the crate and pick it up with F, then face the cart and put it on with F. Salvage with an orange handle can be carried this way all over the island.\n\nEach test uses one of the station's prototype charges. When they run out the station recharges, which takes a while, so measure before you commit.",
            investigate: "Ruler (R): click the cart's starting point, then the marker's centre (yellow pins mark both), and note the horizontal distance. It goes into the notebook (N) under a short name such as d1. Enter that name, or the number with its unit, in the blueprint below. Try the number without a unit first if you like: the station refuses it and says why, and no charge is used.",
            concepts: ["units"],
            requires: [],
            charges: 3,
        ),
        PuzzleDef(
            id: "1.1.1",
            kind: Chase,
            title: "Catch the crawler",
            task: "Send the haul cart to reach the crawler on the transfer marker.",
            why: "That crawler is one of theirs, still walking its rounds, and it carries the sight: the piece their launchers aim with. Without it, nothing here can aim.",
            done: "The sight is on the crane, and the light ran on up the road to the tower. The launchers can aim now, once they know how hard this planet pulls.",
            brief: "A crawler of the road's builders still walks the haul track, round after round, as it may have for a very long time. It carries the sight on its back: the piece the launchers up the road aim with. At the end of each round it waits at its mark, then sets off again at one steady speed, and nothing here can stop or steer it.\n\nThe cart that brought the tools waits behind it, past the stone gate, in the same rail: from here it is the haul cart, and runs at whatever speed you set. When it reaches the crawler the two couple, and only the crane at the transfer marker can lift the sight off. Set the cart's speed so that it reaches the crawler on the marker.\n\nWhen you commit, the crawler sets off on its next round and the cart leaves its mark at the same moment.",
            investigate: "Ruler (R): click the haul cart's front coupler, then the crawler's rear coupler, for the crawler's head start; then the cart's front coupler and the transfer marker's centre. The two couplers meet when the cart reaches the crawler. Click the tops of the two timing posts beside the track for the distance between them.\n\nStopwatch: every run of the experiment below times the crawler from the first timing post to the second and notes it (such as t1). The crawler's speed is not written anywhere: work it out from what you measure.",
            concepts: ["velocity", "relative_velocity"],
            requires: ["1.1.0"],
            rigged: "By evening, a console is wired into the haul track past the stone gate.",
            charges: 3,
            demo: Some(DemoDef(
                question: "The crawler and the haul cart leave their marks at the same moment, the cart at a lower steady speed. A flash marks both at equal moments. How does the gap between them change?",
                choices: [
                    "It grows by the same amount every moment.",
                    "It grows faster and faster.",
                    "It stays the same: both are moving.",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                reveal: "The gap grows by the same amount every moment. Each of them covers equal distances in equal times, and the crawler covers more, so the gap grows at the difference of their speeds. Behind a slower crawler, a faster cart would close it at that difference instead.",
            )),
        ),
        PuzzleDef(
            id: "1.1.2",
            kind: Gravity,
            title: "Local gravity",
            task: "Measure this planet's gravitational acceleration.",
            why: "Their launchers throw exactly as they are told, and telling them means knowing how hard this planet pulls. This backup only carries home's tables. I would rather have your number than my guess.",
            done: "Your g is in me now, and the tower lit all the way up. The sight goes up to the ledge launcher: every shot from here on is worked out with your number.",
            brief: "The launchers do exactly what they are told and work nothing out. Before they can aim, we need the gravitational acceleration here, and the only value I carry is home's.\n\nThe builders' drop tower beside this station holds a test mass. Measure how far it falls and how long that takes. When you commit a value, a second mass is released from the taller survey mast and timed against your prediction.",
            investigate: "Ruler (R): click the release point at the top of the drop tower, then the landing plate below it. Stopwatch: every run of the experiment below times the fall and notes it.\n\nLight gate: the gate on the stand by this station can be carried (F) and clamped on the drop tower at any of the orange notches painted on it a metre apart, from a metre below the release down to the plate: point at the height on the tower and press F. With a gate fitted, each run times the fall from the release to the gate and notes the drop and the time as a pair with one number (such as h2 and t2). The stopwatch reads to hundredths of a second, so a short drop gives a rough value: the longer the drop, the better the reading.",
            concepts: ["acceleration", "free_fall"],
            requires: ["1.1.1"],
            rigged: "By evening, a console is wired into the drop tower.",
            charges: 3,
            demo: Some(DemoDef(
                question: "The test mass is released from the top of the drop tower. A flash marks where it is at equal moments as it falls. How will the marks be spaced?",
                choices: [
                    "Evenly: it falls at a steady speed.",
                    "Further apart lower down: it keeps speeding up.",
                    "Closer together lower down: it slows as it falls.",
                ],
                answer: 1,
                reveal: "The gaps grow all the way down: in each moment the mass falls further than in the moment before. It gains speed at a steady rate, and that rate is the planet's gravitational acceleration.",
            )),
        ),
        PuzzleDef(
            id: "1.1.3",
            kind: Canyon,
            title: "Across the canyon",
            task: "Land the beam's crate on the platform across the canyon.",
            why: "The frame beam itself, crated, across the canyon to the tilting launcher. Our first real shot with your g.",
            done: "The beam made it across, and both rims lit up. It is on the tilting launcher now, pointing at the ridge.",
            brief: "The frame beam, packed in a salvage crate, has to cross the canyon. The builders' launcher sits on the canyon's high rim and fires north, horizontally. The orange strip on the far side, beside the tilting launcher, is where the crate has to land: one block long, so it takes a worked-out shot, not a lucky one.\n\nIts speed dial, worn or built that way, turns only within a band: we found its stops and painted them on the rating plate. From the bare ledge, no speed in that band reaches the strip. The launcher rides in a stone jack frame at the rim, with sockets that take our salvage crates: every crate slid in raises it one metre.\n\nWork out how high the launcher must stand for a speed in the band to do, and build it: pick up a crate on the ledge with F, carry it to the frame and put it in with F (F at the frame takes one back out). Then set the speed for the height you built, and commit.",
            investigate: "Ruler (R): click the launcher's release point, then the centre of the strip. The ruler reports the horizontal and the vertical separation; the release point rises with the launcher. The rating plate on the station gives the dial's band: note it.",
            concepts: ["free_fall", "independence"],
            requires: ["1.1.2"],
            rigged: "By evening, a console is wired into the ledge launcher, up the new steps.",
            charges: 3,
